{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a quiet, pink snow over ancient temples. I spent mornings sipping matcha in hidden teahouses, listening to the rustle of bamboo groves and the distant chime of temple bells. No one rushed. No one shouted. Even the pigeons moved with purpose.\n\nIn Tokyo, the contrast was electric. Shibuya Crossing pulsed like a living organism—thousands of people, each a single note in a symphony of motion. I ate ramen from a 70-year-old stall tucked beneath train tracks, the broth rich as memory, the noodles perfect. At 2 a.m., I wandered Akihabara, bathed in neon, past anime figurines and robot cafés, wondering if I’d stepped into a dream.\n\nI took the bullet train to Nara. Deer, bold and curious, bowed their heads for senbei. One nuzzled my palm like an old friend. At Todai-ji, the Great Buddha sat serene, centuries of silence in his smile.\n\nIn a ryokan outside Hakone, I soaked in an onsen under stars, steam curling into the cool night. My host, an elderly woman with kind eyes, served kaiseki: tiny dishes of seasonal beauty—a single persimmon, a sliver of grilled fish, a leaf of wasabi as sharp as truth.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers. It teaches you to pause. To notice the curve of a teacup. The way light falls on paper lanterns. The quiet dignity of a bow.\n\nI left with no souvenirs except a folded origami crane and a quieter heart.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan.\n\nI remembered how to breathe.\n\n— 297 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.384185506